// Data model: theorems of elementary number theory (MSC 2020 11A) and the premises of one usual textbook proof each.
// Data for the theorem graph theorems.html, drawn with the shared code of the concept graphs.
//
//   theorems id, name, kind, area, field, msc, msc5, premises, formula, text, proof, concepts, year, who.
//            `kind`:     axiom, theorem, lemma, corollary, conjecture (keys in `kinds`).
//            `area`:     five-character MSC 2020 class (`msc5`), the same keys as in data/concepts.js.
//            `premises`: theorems that the proof named in `proof` uses directly. Premises that already follow from
//                        another listed premise are left out. References to other folders carry a prefix
//                        (gt:lagrange-theorem); universal proof principles such as induction are not premises (user, 3 November 2026); only the anchor theorems of those folders.
//            `formula`:  quantifier-free core in LaTeX; quantifiers and hypotheses are in `text`.
//            `concepts`: concepts of data/concepts.js (unprefixed) or of other folders (prefixed) that occur in the
//                        statement.
//            `year`, `who`: first proof (for conjectures: first statement).
//            `premises` and `proof` are the standard proof, the usual short textbook proof; its name is `proofName`
//            (optional `proofYear`, `proofWho`). Further essentially different proofs are in `alternativeProofs`
//            ({ name, premises, idea, year, who }, most important first, never circular). `foundation: true` marks the
//            general tools of the area (division with remainder, Bézout, Euclid's lemma); the card does not expand
//            their premises (user, 3 November 2026).
//
// Prefix for references from other folders: "ent:<id>".
// Sources: Hardy and Wright, An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers (6th ed. 2008); Niven, Zuckerman and
// Montgomery, An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers (5th ed. 1991); Ireland and Rosen, A Classical Introduction to
// Modern Number Theory (2nd ed. 1990). Starting material: the 11A theorems of maps/karte-1830 and maps/karte-1900.
// Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory. tests/check-theorems.js warns about theorems without
// premises that have neither mark.

